{"page": 129, "text": "During the third quarter of 2026, macroeconomic conditions reflected persistent inflation, a slowdown in job growth and continued volatility in energy prices and interest rates amid the ongoing conflict with Iran. The Federal Reserve maintained the federal funds target range at 3.50%\u20133.75% at its July 2026 meeting and then raised the range by 25 basis points to 3.75%\u20134.00% at its September 2026 meeting, its first increase since 2023 and the first rate change under Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh. The accompanying Summary of Economic Projections indicated the potential for an additional rate increase later in 2026, a reversal from the cutting-cycle expectations that had prevailed as recently as the first quarter. 97\nHeadline inflation increased further during the quarter, primarily reflecting higher energy prices, as West Texas Intermediate crude oil prices rose approximately 30% during the quarter, from $69.50 per barrel at the end of June 2026 to $90.42 per barrel at the end of September 2026. Core Consumer Price Index (\u201cCPI\u201d) was roughly stable.\nMarket interest rates increased sharply during the quarter, with the 10-year Treasury yield rising 85 basis points to 5.29%, while market expectations continued to reflect the possibility of a further rate increase later in 2026. Equity markets advanced modestly during the quarter, with the S&P 500 gaining 2.0%, following a 14.9% gain in the second quarter of 2026.\nInflation\nInflation increased further during the third quarter of 2026, primarily reflecting higher energy prices amid the ongoing conflict with Iran. CPI inflation rose from 3.5% in June 2026 to 3.7% in September 2026, driven in part by an increase in energy prices from 15.7% in June 2026 to 20.4% in September 2026 on a year-over-year basis.\nCore CPI, which excludes food and energy, remained essentially flat at 2.6% in September 2026. Core Personal Consumption Expenditures, the Federal Reserve\u2019s preferred measure of underlying inflation, increased 3.1% year-over-year in September 2026, compared to 3.0% year-over-year in June 2026. Other inflation indicators were mixed, with producer price inflation rising to 6.3% in September 2026 from 5.6% in June 2026, and import prices increasing 8.1% over the 12 months ending September 30, 2026, compared to 6.7% over the 12 months ending June 30, 2026.\nTreasury Yields\nTreasury yields increased sharply during the third quarter of 2026. The 10-year Treasury yield rose 85 basis points to 5.29% from 4.44% at the end of June 2026. Shorter-term yields also increased, though by less, with the 2-year Treasury yield rising 74 basis points to 4.88%. As a result, the yield curve steepened modestly, with the spread between 2-year and 10-year Treasury yields widening from 30 basis points to 41 basis points over the quarter. This shift reflects the Federal Reserve\u2019s September rate increase and the more hawkish policy outlook communicated alongside it, together with persistent inflation and elevated energy prices.\n"}
{"page": 130, "text": "Labor Markets\nLabor market conditions reflected the low-hire, low-fire environment during the third quarter of 2026, with slower job growth and a stable unemployment rate. The unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.2% in September 2026 compared to June 2026. Job growth slowed during the quarter, with non-farm payrolls increasing by an average of 51,000 per month, compared to an average of 81,000 per month during the second quarter of 2026. However, initial unemployment insurance claims declined, averaging 200,000 per week during the third quarter of 2026, compared to 222,000 per week in the prior quarter.\nHousing Market\nHousing market activity softened during the third quarter of 2026. Existing home sales declined to an annualized rate of 3.99 million, compared to 4.13 million in the second quarter of 2026, amid sharply higher mortgage rates. New home sales declined to a seasonally adjusted annualized rate of approximately 676,000 in September 2026, compared to approximately 680,000 in the second quarter of 2026. Home price growth slowed, with the median resale price rising 1.3% year-over-year in September 2026, compared to 2.3% in June 2026. Mortgage rates increased sharply during the quarter, with the 30-year fixed rate rising to 7.10% from 6.29% at the end of June 2026.\nCommercial Real Estate\nThe U.S. CRE market moved through the third quarter of 2026 with improving fundamentals in several sectors, even as the interest rate backdrop became less supportive. In September, the Federal Open Market Committee raised the federal funds target range by 25 basis points to 3.75%\u20134.00%, its first increase since 2023, and updated projections point to the possibility of an additional increase later this year\u2014a reversal from the cutting-cycle expectations that prevailed as recently as the first quarter. Longer-term rates moved sharply higher, with the 10-year Treasury yield rising 85 basis points to 5.29%, as persistent inflation and geopolitical developments affecting energy prices added further inflation risk. Despite this, capital has continued to flow into the sector, with underwriting simply reflecting a more disciplined, higher-for-longer rate environment rather than a retreat from CRE broadly.\nThe economic conditions discussed above influence our investment strategy and results.\n98\nThe following table summarizes the change in U.S. gross domestic product (\u201cGDP\u201d) estimates (annualized rate) according to the U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis:\nThree Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n2026 June 30,\n2026 March 31,\n2026 December 31,\n2025 September 30,\n2025\nReal GDP 3.4 % 2.2 % 2.5 % 0.5 % 4.4 %\nThe following table summarizes the annualized U.S. unemployment rate according to the U.S. Department of Labor:\nSeptember 30,\n2026 June 30,\n2026 March 31,\n2026 December 31,\n2025 September 30,\n2025\nUnemployment rate 4.2 % 4.2 % 4.3 % 4.4 % 4.4 %\n"}
