ormation, including a summary of activity related to consumer loans from December 31, 2025 to September 30, 2026.\n112\nSingle-Family Rental Properties\nAs of September 30, 2026, our SFR portfolio consisted of approximately 3,735 properties with an aggregate carrying value of approximately $0.9 billion, of which $0.3 billion were classified as held-for-sale and $0.6 billion were classified as held-for-investment. The properties classified as held for investment did not meet all of the criteria for held for sale classification. We will reclassify applicable properties when those criteria are met, at which point they would be measured at the lower of carrying value or fair value less costs to sell, which could result in the recognition of impairment charges that may be material.\n"}
{"page": 150, "text": "In connection with our decision to wind down our SFR business, we are pursuing the sale of some or all of our SFR properties. Pending disposition, we expect to continue operating and managing the portfolio, generating rental income and incurring related operating expenses. Operating results are influenced by rental rates, occupancy levels, tenant turnover and local market conditions. Our SFR properties are financed through a combination of credit facilities, term loans and securitization structures. The wind-down could affect our operating results and liquidity, including through the repayment or refinancing of related debt and associated costs. See Note 17 to our consolidated financial statements for additional information regarding the financing of our SFR properties.\nOur Investment Portfolio segment also includes results from certain wholly owned subsidiaries and minority investments that provide services across the mortgage and real estate sectors. This includes our strategic partnership with Darwin through Adoor Property Management LLC (\u201cAPM\u201d), which provides property management services. All of our SFR properties are currently managed by APM.\nCommercial Real Estate\nFollowing the acquisition of Elecor in December 2025, we own and operate a portfolio of Class A office properties in New York City and San Francisco, which are managed as part of our broader real estate platform, totaling approximately 12.2 million square feet.\nThe key metrics related to Elecor CRE properties are included in the tables below.\nQuarterly Leasing Information:\nThree Months Ended Nine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\n2026 June 30,\n2026 September 30,\n2026\nLease count 10 16 35\nSq ft leased (total) 426,361 234,347 763,851\nRithm Capital\u2019s share (sq ft) 259,168 131,571 458,171\nWeighted average initial rent ($ per sq ft) $ 109.14 $ 92.90 $ 102.56\nWeighted average lease term (years) 10.0 8.9 9.5\nSame Store Leased Occupancy:\nSeptember 30,\n2026 December 31, 2025\nNew York City(A) 88.6 % 91.7 %\nSan Francisco 65.0 % 62.2 %\nTotal(B) 83.5 % 85.3 %\n(A)New York City same store leased occupancy as of December 31, 2025 was 92.8% as originally reported and was recalculated to 91.7% using Elecor\u2019s ownership interests as of September 30, 2026 to reflect the partial sale of an interest in a CRE joint venture. (B)Same store leased occupancy as of December 31, 2025 was 86.9% as originally reported and was recalculated to 85.3% using Elecor\u2019s ownership interests as of September 30, 2026 to reflect the partial sale of an interest in a CRE joint venture.\n"}
{"page": 151, "text": "Same store leased occupancy includes the core properties owned and operated by Elecor in a similar manner throughout both periods, and excludes properties acquired, disposed of, or under development or redevelopment during either period. Leased occupancy for all periods presented is calculated using Elecor\u2019s ownership interests as of September 30, 2026, and prior periods were recalculated to reflect the partial sale of an interest in a CRE joint venture in September 2026.\nSame store leased occupancy was 83.5% as of September 30, 2026, compared to 85.3% as of December 31, 2025, a decrease of 180 basis points. This decrease was driven primarily by lease expirations in the nine months, partially offset by new leases executed across the portfolio. Year-to-date leasing activity has been executed at rents 32.6% above the full-year 2025 average, reflecting improving tenant demand for premier Class A office space across the Elecor portfolio. 113\nLease Expiration Schedule:\nYear of Expiration Expiring Sq Ft(A) % of Total Sq Ft Annualized Base Rent (in thousands)(A) % of Total Portfolio Annualized Base Rent\nOctober 1 through December 31, 2026 137,229 2.3 % $ 10,239 2.0 %\n2027 165,895 2.8 % 16,139 3.1 %\n2028 178,262 3.0 % 14,663 2.9 %\n2029 397,970 6.8 % 33,510 6.5 %\n2030 497,143 8.4 % 49,690 9.6 %\n2031 and thereafter 4,520,157 76.7 % 391,489 75.9 %\nTotal 5,896,656 100.0 % $ 515,730 100.0 %\n(A) Amounts are presented at Rithm Capital share.\nCRITICAL ACCOUNTING POLICIES AND USE OF ESTIMATES\nManagement\u2019s Discussion and Analysis of Financial Condition and Results of Operations is based upon our consolidated financial statements, which have been prepared in accordance with U.S. generally accepted accounting principles (\u201cGAAP\u201d). The preparation of financial statements in conformity with GAAP requires the use of estimates and assumptions that could affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities, the disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities and the reported amounts of revenue and expenses. Actual results could differ from these estimates. We believe that these estimates and assumptions utilized in the preparation of the consolidated financial statements are prudent and reasonable. Actual results historically have generally been in line with our estimates and judgments used in applying each of the accounting policies described below, as modified periodically to reflect current market conditions.\n"}
{"page": 152, "text": "The mortgage and financial sectors operate in a challenging and uncertain economic environment. Financial and real estate companies continue to be affected by, among other things, market volatility, heightened interest rates and inflationary pressures. We believe the estimates and assumptions underlying our consolidated financial statements are reasonable and supportable based on the information available as of September 30, 2026; however, uncertainty over the current macroeconomic conditions makes any estimates and assumptions as of September 30, 2026, inherently less certain than they would be absent the current economic environment. Actual results may materially differ from those estimates. Market volatility and inflationary pressures and their impact on the current financial, economic and capital markets environment and future developments in these and other areas present uncertainty and risk with respect to our financial condition, results of operations, liquidity and ability to pay distributions.\nOur critical accounting policies as of September 30, 2026, which represent our accounting policies that are most affected by judgments, estimates and assumptions, included all of the critical accounting policies referred to in the 2025 Form 10-K.\nRECENT ACCOUNTING PRONOUNCEMENTS\nSee Note 2 to our consolidated financial statements in this Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q. 114\nRESULTS OF OPERATIONS\nFactors Impacting Comparability of Our Results of Operations\nOur net income is primarily generated from net interest income, servicing fee revenue less cost to service, gain on sale of loans less cost to originate, asset management fees less expenses and property rental revenue less operating costs. Changes in various factors such as market interest rates, prepayment speeds, estimated future cash flows, servicing costs and credit quality could affect the amount of basis premium to be amortized or discount to be accreted into interest income for a given period. Prepayment speeds vary according to the type of investment, conditions in the financial markets, competition and other factors, none of which can be predicted with any certainty. Additionally, changes in these inputs along with other factors such as delinquency rates and recapture rates may significantly impact the fair value of our MSRs and as a result, our earnings. Our operating results may also be affected by credit losses in excess of initial estimates or unanticipated credit events experienced by borrowers whose mortgage loans underlie the MSRs, RTLs or the non-Agency securities held in our investment portfolio. Asset management fees are directly related to growth in AUM and investment performance of our funds. Decline in investment performance may slow our AUM growth and increase the potential for redemptions from our funds. Property rental revenue is directly related to occupancy.\n"}
